Sval: the crash dialog can be reached from the Dock and Cmd-Tab

The crash reporter's Info.plist sets LSUIElement, because the same binary
also runs without a window (--ping-cleanup, --send-ping), and those runs
must not put an icon in the Dock. It applies to the dialog too, so once
another window covers it, neither the Dock nor Cmd-Tab can bring it back,
and it waits there for the user. The run that shows a window switches to
the regular activation policy before it activates.

diff --git a/toolkit/crashreporter/client/app/src/ui/macos/mod.rs b/toolkit/crashreporter/client/app/src/ui/macos/mod.rs
index e7503bcde8..dc1adc55c1 100644
--- a/toolkit/crashreporter/client/app/src/ui/macos/mod.rs
+++ b/toolkit/crashreporter/client/app/src/ui/macos/mod.rs
@@ -259,6 +259,12 @@ objc_class! {
     impl AppDelegate: NSObject /*<NSApplicationDelegate>*/ {
         #[sel(applicationDidFinishLaunching:)]
         fn application_did_finish_launching(&mut self, _notification: Ptr<cocoa::NSNotification>) {
+            // Info.plist's LSUIElement keeps the windowless runs out of the Dock. The run that shows
+            // a window takes the regular policy, so the Dock and Cmd-Tab can bring its window back.
+            unsafe {
+                cocoa::NSApplication::sharedApplication()
+                    .setActivationPolicy_(cocoa::NSApplicationActivationPolicyRegular)
+            };
             // Activate the application (bringing windows to the active foreground later)
             unsafe { cocoa::NSApplication::sharedApplication().activateIgnoringOtherApps_(runtime::YES) };
 
